# Golden files are compared byte-for-byte against strings that tests build with
# "\n". With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default, and what this repo is
# developed on) git rewrites them to CRLF on checkout, so the comparison fails
# on line endings alone while every line matches. That is what broke
# TestToFacts_EmittedPredicateArity_ShouldMatchGolden: 25 identical lines, 25
# CRLF pairs, one failing test.
#
# Pinning eol=lf keeps golden files byte-stable on every platform.
#
# Note: .gitignore line 20 ignores all dotfiles ("(.*)"), which catches this
# file too, so it had to be added with "git add -f".
*.golden text eol=lf

# The dead-code baseline is compared line by line; pin eol so a Windows
# checkout does not read every entry as changed.
scripts/testdata/deadcode-baseline.txt text eol=lf

# cmd/nerd/chat/persona.go carries the architect's persona as a multi-line raw
# string literal, and TestPersonaWordingUnchanged compares that literal
# byte-for-byte against testdata/architect_persona.golden (pinned to LF above).
# With core.autocrlf=true every newline INSIDE the backticks would become CRLF
# on checkout while the golden stayed LF, so the two would differ on line
# endings alone and the gate on the architect's own words would go red for a
# reason that has nothing to do with his words. Pin the source file too.
cmd/nerd/chat/persona.go text eol=lf
